PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The poem is a double sestina with recurring end-words mountains, valleys, forests, music, morning, evening. The body’s end-word rotation is otherwise intact, so any swapped pair must share an end-word; this eliminates 28&74, 45&47, and 25&73, which mix different terminal words.

My current position remains 25 and 74. The decisive evidence is the envoi: lines 73–75 should gather the six end-words in pairs. Line 73 has mountains/valleys and line 75 has morning/evening, so line 74 should supply forests/music. Line 25, “These forests eke, made wretched by our music,” fits that exact slot. Swapping 25 and 74 puts “Long since, alas, my deadly swannish music” at line 25, which gives line 26’s singular “Hath made itself…” a clear antecedent and repairs the awkward plural “These forests… Hath” construction. It also restores the envoi to: mountains/valleys, forests/music, morning/evening.

The main competing answer is 4 and 11. Those lines are near-duplicates both ending in music, differing only in “your” vs “you.” Colleagues favoring it argue this is the minimal same-slot corruption and preserves the end-word rotation. I think it is more likely a textual variant or red herring: swapping them does not repair the envoi or the grammar at 25–26, and the “your/you” difference may be original or typographical rather than evidence of a line swap. Other candidates: 25&68 still leaves the envoi missing forests/music and disrupts the “For she…” context; 28&74 and 45&47 break the end-word sequence; 24&74 does not supply the needed forests/music pairing; 50&58 shares evening but resolves no clear formal or grammatical break. Current deliberation split: many colleagues now support 25&74, while a persistent minority supports 4&11. My belief remains 25 and 74.}

