PRIVATE REFLECTION: {I remain confident the swapped lines are **25 and 74**.

The poem is a double sestina with recurring end-words in the order **mountains, valleys, forests, music, morning, evening**. The end-word rotation is otherwise intact, so any swapped pair must share a terminal word. The decisive evidence is the closing envoi, lines 73–75. In the intended form, the envoi gathers the six end-words in pairs: line 73 should supply **mountains/valleys**, line 75 supplies **morning/evening**, and line 74 must therefore supply **forests/music**. Line 25, “These forests eke, made wretched by our music,” is exactly that missing envoi line. Swapping 25 and 74 gives:

73 These mountains witness shall, so shall these valleys;  
74 These forests eke, made wretched by our music;  
75 Our morning hymn is this, and song at evening.

This also repairs the grammar at 25–26. After the swap, line 25 becomes “Long since, alas, my deadly swannish music,” whose singular **music** naturally governs line 26’s “Hath made itself a crier of the morning.” In the modified text, “These forests … Hath made itself” is awkward because “forests” is plural.

The main rival is **4 and 11**, the near-identical invocations ending in **music** and differing only in “your” vs. “you.” Colleagues favoring it call this the most concrete same-slot residue of a swap. However, it is weak: swapping 4 and 11 does not restore two clean parallel forms if the original had “your” in both places; it likely points to a textual variant/typo rather than the intended transposition. It also leaves the broken envoi and the 25–26 grammar unresolved. Several colleagues in round 2 noted this fatal weakness: the swap cannot eliminate the “your/you” mismatch unless the original itself had mixed pronouns, and it does nothing to reconstruct the required **forests/music** envoi pairing.

Other proposed pairs are weaker: 28&74 fails because line 28 ends in **forests**, not **music**, and the envoi expects **forests/music** in line 74; 25&68 and 24&74 do not supply the needed **forests/music** pairing in the envoi and disrupt surrounding context; 45&47 and 25&73 break the end-word rotation; 50&58 shares **evening** but repairs no clear formal or grammatical break.

Current round split is roughly 8 for **25 and 74** and 6 for **4 and 11**, with 25/74 gaining support from the envoi completion plus grammatical repair. My belief remains **25 and 74**.}

