I remain with **25 and 74**.

The decisive point is the closing envoi. In the intended double sestina, lines 73–75 gather the six recurring end-words in pairs: 73 gives **mountains/valleys**, 75 gives **morning/evening**, so 74 must supply **forests/music**. Only line 25, “These forests eke, made wretched by our music,” does that. Swapping it into 74 restores the envoi exactly:

> 73 These mountains witness shall, so shall these valleys;  
> 74 These forests eke, made wretched by our music;  
> 75 Our morning hymn is this, and song at evening.

This swap also repairs the grammar at 25–26: after the swap, line 25 becomes “Long since, alas, my deadly swannish music,” whose singular **music** naturally governs line 26’s “Hath made itself…”. The 4/11 proposal does not repair that, and swapping two lines that already both end in **music** only moves the “your/you” variant rather than resolving it. It leaves the envoi broken, while 25/74 completes the formal structure. So my answer remains **25 and 74**.

