3.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: I remain with **25 and 74**.

The decisive evidence is still the double-sestina envoi. The final three lines must gather the six recurring end-words in pairs: line 73 supplies **mountains/valleys**, and line 75 supplies **morning/evening**. Therefore line 74 must supply the remaining pair, **forests/music**. Only line 25 does that:

> These forests eke, made wretched by our music;

Swapping 25 and 74 restores the envoi:

> 73 These mountains witness shall, so shall these valleys;  
> 74 These forests eke, made wretched by our music;  
> 75 Our morning hymn is this, and song at evening.

The swap also repairs lines 25–26. After the exchange, line 25 becomes “Long since, alas, my deadly swannish music,” and the singular **music** naturally governs line 26’s “Hath made itself a crier of the morning.” In the given text, “These forests … Hath made itself” is syntactically awkward.

On the rival **4 and 11** proposal: the new argument that the swap restores speaker-appropriate pronoun distribution is not persuasive. “Vouchsafe you silent ears” can be read as an imperative with an expressed subject (“do you vouchsafe…”), so it need not be a corruption of “your.” More importantly, swapping 4 and 11 does not repair the malformed envoi or the 25–26 syntax; it merely exchanges a local variant. The structural break the puzzle asks us to identify is the misplaced envoi line, so my answer remains **25 and 74**.

